## 4. `ClientPackage` (purchased wallet instance)

**File:** `/opt/soulpilates/SoulPilates.Core/Entities/ClientPackage.cs`

```csharp
public class ClientPackage : BaseEntity
{
    [BsonRepresentation(BsonType.ObjectId)]
    public string ClientId { get; set; } = null!;
    public string? InvoiceLineId { get; set; }
    public string Format { get; set; } = "GROUP"; // "GROUP", "PRIVATE", "FREELANCE" (P3-S13 — freelance package slots)
    public int TotalCredits { get; set; }
    public int CreditsUsed { get; set; }
    public int CreditsRemaining { get; set; }
    public DateTime ActivationDate { get; set; }
    public DateTime? ExpiryDate { get; set; }
    public string Status { get; set; } = "ACTIVE"; // "ACTIVE", "LOW_BALANCE", "COMPLETED", "EXPIRED", "FROZEN"
    public decimal FractionalValuePerClass { get; set; }
    public List<string> RetentionRemindersSent { get; set; } = new();
    public string? SourceNote { get; set; }
}
```

- `Format` is a **single string**, one value per wallet, matched by simple string equality/`In()` against `CalendarSession.Format` at spend time.
- **`Format` is populated directly from `Product.FormatRestriction`** at grant/purchase time — confirmed at `WalletService.cs:638` (`GrantPackageAsync`): `Format = product.FormatRestriction,` and similarly in `InvoiceService.cs:527` and `PaymentService.cs`. So **the `ClientPackage.Format` comment (`"GROUP", "PRIVATE", "FREELANCE"`) is once again stale** — since `Product.FormatRestriction` can be `MAT`/`DUO`/`MIXED`/`NONE` too, `ClientPackage.Format` inherits all of those in practice.
- **This is directly confirmed by `WalletService.cs:79-88`** (`GetActivePackagesForClientAsync`), which is written assuming `ClientPackage.Format` can be `DUO`:
  ```csharp
  // MAT is a sub-type of group class. A MAT session can be paid with either a
  // MAT-specific package (preferred — consumed first) or a generic GROUP package
  // (fallback). Exact matches on other formats (PRIVATE, DUO, FREELANCE) are kept
  // strict so packages are never cross-consumed across incompatible class types.
  var upper = (format ?? "").ToUpperInvariant();
  var formatFilter = upper == "MAT"
      ? Builders<ClientPackage>.Filter.In(p => p.Format, new[] { "MAT", "GROUP" })
      : Builders<ClientPackage>.Filter.Eq(p => p.Format, upper == "" ? format : upper);
  ```
- **No headcount/party-size/seats/duo field exists on `ClientPackage`** beyond the single `Format` string — I grepped the full entity (above) and there is nothing resembling `Seats`, `PartySize`, `Headcount`, `PeoplePerCredit`, etc. A "how many people per credit" concept does **not** exist here; instead, the current Duo design spends **2 credits for 2 people** (see §5 `Quantity`), i.e. it's modeled at the `Attendance.Quantity` level, not at the wallet level.
- `AnalyticsService.cs:2245-2253` documents the credit "bucket" mapping used at forecast/report time: `PRIVATE` and `DUO` sessions both draw on the **`PRIVATE`** credit bucket; `GROUP`/`MAT` draw on `GROUP`. So even though `ClientPackage.Format` can literally equal `"DUO"`, session-side deduction logic collapses `DUO`→`PRIVATE` for bucket purposes in some analytics code — but **not** in `WalletService.GetActivePackagesForClientAsync`, which treats `DUO` as its own strict bucket (potential inconsistency to flag for the schema-design decision).

